{"parentUuid":"aa718ed2-e86f-4a18-b951-6cd46210f940","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0e75e1c2-9f84-43c4-8006-cccad7102c77","agentId":"a11c0a040e3cf682b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016CbxG7CQUVKuyx9sMrKaam","type":"tool_result","content":"the following rules refused this work:\n  - Cursor: the statement says every lifecycle hook receives the working directory, but Cursor's common schema (cursor.com/docs/hooks#common-schema) lists only `workspace_roots`, not `cwd`; its cited runs show `cwd` only on tool/shell events (9 of 27 payloads in tool-failure, none on sessionStart or afterAgentThought) and `transcript_path` is null throughout, and no deviation records this.\n  - codex: the statement says exit 2 uses the hook stderr as the message only when the hook gave no other reason, but codex-mock/snapshots/runs/hook-exit-json (echo h: JSON deny reason \"the JSON reason wins\" with stderr \"the stderr loses\" on exit 2) shows the real harness blocking with the stderr text (\"Command blocked by PreToolUse hook: the stderr loses\"), so the codex cell needs a deviation for it.\n  - Cursor: the statement's 'whatever it printed is discarded' part is neither stated by the doc (only 'timeout | Execution timeout in seconds') nor shown by cursor-mock/snapshots/runs/hook-timeout, because setup/slow.sh prints its deny only after `sleep 5`, so it never prints anything before the 1s timeout kills it; the run shows the grandchild killed and the event carrying on, but not discarded output, and the cell lists no deviation for it (Codex's run does show this, since its hook prints the deny before the timeout).\n  - Cursor's cell never grounds the statement's part 'the same hook configured in several settings files runs once': its doc (cursor.com/docs/hooks#configuration) says only 'All matching hooks from every source run, and Cursor merges their responses' (it even reads against dedup), no cited run configures one hook in two files, and the deviation only says that case is 'not modeled' by the mock rather than that Cursor does not do it, so the part is neither stated, shown, nor listed as a harness deviation.\n  - The statement part \"Whatever the hook prints is not recorded in the transcript\" is neither documented nor shown for codex (doc says only \"advisory, so their output won't steer Codex\"; the run hook.sh prints nothing to stdout, it only appends to HOOK_LOG) nor for cursor (doc says \"The response is logged but not used\"; the pretool-refusal hook.sh prints nothing for sessionEnd), and it is not listed as a deviation.\n  - Codex is listed as supported with no deviations, but the statement says the Stop payload carries the background tasks still running, and neither the Codex doc (Stop table lists only turn_id, stop_hook_active, last_assistant_message) nor the codex stops run payloads (Stop payloads have only those fields, no background-task list) show it; the cell needs a deviation for background tasks.\n  - Codex's doc (UserPromptSubmit section) and its two cited runs (hook-exit-codes, prompt-blocked) show adding context and blocking, but nothing states or shows that the hook cannot rewrite the prompt or that it fires for a background-task notification (the runs only submit a plain user prompt), and the codex cell lists no deviations for those parts.\n  - background-agent/cursor: the recording shows the sub-agent firing preToolUse Shell (with timeout 30000) and postToolUse Shell (tool_output) under its own session_id, but cursor-mock/e2e/001_hooks/background_agent_test.go only inspects beforeShellExecution/afterShellExecution (and the Task preToolUse), so a mock that never fires the sub-agent's preToolUse/postToolUse hooks would still pass. (file-guard \"capability-rigor\")\n  - cursor: the recorded tool-failure run (shell command printing only to stderr, `sh -c 'echo OOPS >&2; exit 3'`, failure message \"OOPS\") is not driven by any test marked sr:proves bash-tool-result/cursor; TestAShellCommandExitingNonZeroIsAFailureResultWithItsOutputOrExitCode replays only shell-exit-status (no stderr-only case), and the tool-failure replay is claimed for file-tools/cursor, so the stderr-only variant has no explicit assertion.\n  - hook-command-handler/codex: TestCommandHookReadsThePayloadOnStdinInTheSessionDirectory only runs hooks with the session started at the repo root and a pwd/pipe command line, so it cannot tell 'session cwd' (hooks#config-shape: 'Commands run with the session cwd as their working directory', 'Codex may be started from a subdirectory') from the git root or project root; it never starts a session in a subdirectory, and none of the codex tests exercises the recorded hook form (command substitution '\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\"/hook.sh') as a hook-command-handler assertion. Add a test with the session cwd in a subdirectory (hook pwd == that subdirectory) and a $(...) command line.\n  - cursor: the recorded tool-failure payloads carry a cwd field (cwd is the empty string on preToolUse and beforeShellExecution, and the docs list cwd on preToolUse), but TestEveryHookPayloadNamesTheSessionTheTranscriptAndTheWorkspace asserts only session_id, conversation_id, workspace_roots and transcript_path, so no test checks the cwd the mock puts in tool and shell payloads. The codex subject checks out.\n  - hook-matcher-filter/cursor: the doc states that an empty-string matcher matches everything and that afterShellExecution is matched on the command text (and postToolUseFailure on the tool name), but the only test file (cursor-mock/e2e/001_hooks/matcher_timeout_test.go) and the hook-matchers recording use only '*' and an omitted matcher, so no test covers an empty matcher, an afterShellExecution matcher, or a postToolUseFailure matcher. Related codex gap: the doc says to omit matcher entirely to match everything, but no test uses a hook with the matcher omitted (only '*' and ''). The codex tests otherwise match the recording: session-startup ran and resume did not, pre-star, pre-bash and pre-empty ran while pre-edit-write and pre-nomatch did not, the UserPromptSubmit and Stop matchers were ignored, and the apply_patch aliases are covered by the unit test.\n  - noninteractive-run/cursor: the headless doc (#how-it-works) says print mode only proposes file changes unless --force/--yolo is given, but the mock accepts and ignores --force/--yolo (cursor-mock/root.go), no test runs without --force, and the cursor deviation covers only output formats and --stream-partial-output, so this documented variant is neither tested nor declared.\n  - posttooluse-payload/cursor: the only listed test (custom_test.go TestAfterAToolSucceeds...) replays just file-tools and only asserts tool_input/tool_output NotEmpty and duration is a float, so it does not pin tool_output (Write {\"file_path\",\"success\":true}, Read {\"file_path\",\"content_length\":3}) or tool_name. It never covers the recorded Shell postToolUse (tool_output {\"output\":\"FINE\n  - session-end-hook/codex: the doc says SessionEnd won't run for subagents (main thread only), and the mock has a subagent path (codex-mock/internal/runner/spawn_agent.go), but no test spawns a subagent and asserts no SessionEnd hook fires for it; the doc's archive/delete/idle-30-minute triggers are also neither tested nor declared as a deviation in the spec. (file-guard \"capability-rigor\")\n  - codex TestTranscriptIsADayAndSessionIDFileThatExistsAtStart checks the sessions/YYYY/MM/DD/rollout-<timestamp>-<id>.jsonl layout only by regex shape, never that the YYYY/MM/DD directory equals the date in the rollout filename (recorded: sessions/2026/10/03/rollout-2026-10-03T17-00-39-...), so a mock filing the rollout under the wrong day, which the deviation says is the day it started, would still pass.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8662b5ca-ccf2-4387-b5b1-366c6d19d3d7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:34:52.294Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"aa718ed2-e86f-4a18-b951-6cd46210f940","userType":"external","entryp